::ENG2_U01_L04_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The school added a homework help table in the cafeteria before first period. Students could ask quick questions before class began. Question: Which response best explains how the help table supported students?{
~A. The table was in the cafeteria and school started later.#Incorrect. This mentions the place but does not explain the support.
=B. The homework help table supported students because they could ask quick questions before class.#Correct. This answers the question and uses evidence from the passage.
~C. Students had homework, so the cafeteria was helpful.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not use the passage evidence clearly.
~D. The school had tables and questions.#Incorrect. This is too vague to be an evidence-based response.
}

::ENG2_U01_L04_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The park added signs showing the length of each trail loop. Families used the signs to choose walks that matched the time they had available. Question: Which sentence is the best explanation?{
~A. The families walked because signs were outside.#Incorrect. This does not explain how the signs helped.
~B. The park had trails, and trails are good.#Incorrect. This is too vague and does not use evidence.
~C. The signs were in the park because families had time.#Incorrect. This confuses the relationship between the signs and the families' choices.
=D. The signs helped families choose a trail because the signs showed the length of each loop.#Correct. This response paraphrases the benefit and includes relevant evidence.
}

::ENG2_U01_L04_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The teacher gave students a checklist for revising paragraphs. The checklist reminded students to check topic sentences, evidence, and punctuation. Question: Which response is clearest?{
=A. The revision checklist helped students improve paragraphs by reminding them to check topic sentences, evidence, and punctuation.#Correct. This response is clear, accurate, and evidence-based.
~B. The checklist was a thing students used.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not explain the checklist's purpose.
~C. Students had paragraphs, so the teacher made them better.#Incorrect. This is unclear and does not use the passage evidence accurately.
~D. The teacher gave something because students wrote.#Incorrect. This is too vague for an academic written response.
}

::ENG2_U01_L04_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The community center opened a quiet computer room for job applications. Visitors could complete forms without noise from the main lobby. Question: Which response includes an accurate paraphrase and evidence?{
~A. The computer room was quiet, and forms exist.#Incorrect. This mentions details but does not explain the benefit.
~B. The lobby was noisy, so nobody completed anything.#Incorrect. The passage does not say nobody completed forms.
=C. The quiet computer room helped visitors complete job applications because they could work without lobby noise.#Correct. This response accurately paraphrases the benefit and includes evidence.
~D. The community center had computers near a room.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not answer the question.
}

::ENG2_U01_L04_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage: The science teacher used a slow-motion video of a bouncing ball. Students could see details in the ball's movement that were too fast to notice in real time. Question: Which response best explains the benefit of the video?{
~A. The video was slow, and students watched it.#Incorrect. This is true but too vague.
=B. The slow-motion video helped students observe movement details that happened too quickly to see in real time.#Correct. This response paraphrases the benefit and uses evidence from the passage.
~C. The ball bounced because science happened.#Incorrect. This does not explain the benefit.
~D. The teacher used a video instead of class.#Incorrect. The passage does not say the video replaced class.
}

